{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01204 - MONDO_18434",
  "verbalization": "Mitoxantrone is a well-established chemotherapeutic agent with a known indication for treating Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and Myelodysplastic Syndromes (MDS) related to topoisomerase type 2 inhibitors. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, all of which point to a shared mechanism of action involving the inhibition of topoisomerase II, a critical enzyme in DNA replication and repair. The evidence suggests that Mitoxantrone may be repurposed for AML with the t(9;11)(p22;q23) translocation, as it is indicated in both Therapy-Related AML and AML with topoisomerase II inhibitor-related MDS. The presence of multiple indirect pathways linking Mitoxantrone to AML with the specific translocation highlights a potential role in targeting the disease through topoisomerase II inhibition. The consistent indication across different pathways supports the hypothesis that Mitoxantrone could be a viable therapeutic option for this subset of AML patients.",
